{:edb/db-before-t 1105 :edb/db-after-t 1106 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534418 50 #inst "2026-10-07T02:14:01.518Z" 13194139534418 true] [17592186078213 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_\\pi^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,\\textrm d x$." 13194139534418 false] [17592186078213 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_\\pi^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" 13194139534418 true] [17592186078214 1100 "Given that ${\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6} \\, f(x) \\; \\textrm d x = -54 },$ what is the value of\n${\\displaystyle \\int_{6}^{0} \\bigg( \\dfrac 1 3 f(x) + 1 \\bigg) \\; \\textrm d x }?$" 13194139534418 true] [17592186078214 1100 "Given that ${\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6} \\, f(x) \\; \\textrm d x = -54}$, what is the value of\n${\\displaystyle \\int_{6}^{0} \\bigg(\\dfrac 1 3 f(x) + 1 \\bigg) \\; \\textrm d x}$?" 13194139534418 false] [17592186078214 1145 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{0}(\\frac{1}{3}f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{6}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{6}^{0}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 54+x\\,{|}_{6}^{0} \\\\  & =18+(0-6) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534418 true] [17592186078214 1145 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n∫_{6}^{0}f(x)dx = - ∫_{0}^{6}f(x)dx = 54\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{6}^{0}(\\frac{1}{3}f(x) + 1)dx &= \\frac{1}{3}∫_{6}^{0}f(x)dx + ∫_{6}^{0}1dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 54 + x \\mid _{6}^{0} \\\\\n&= 18 + (0 - 6) \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534418 false] [17592186078218 1100 "If $\\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 18$ and $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = -6$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{10} \\big( 2f(x)+1 \\big) \\,\\textrm d x?$" 13194139534418 true] [17592186078218 1100 "If $\\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 18$ and $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = -6$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{10} \\big(2f(x)+1 \\big) \\,\\textrm d x$?" 13194139534418 false] [17592186078218 1145 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x + \\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\displaystyle \\int_0^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n-6 + 18 = ∫_{0}^{10}f(x)dx; 12 = ∫_{0}^{10}f(x)dx\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{10}(2f(x) + 1)dx &= 2∫_{0}^{10}f(x)dx + ∫_{0}^{10}dx \\\\\n&= 2(12) + x \\mid _{0}^{10} \\\\\n&= 24 + (10 - 0) \\\\\n&= 34\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534418 false] [17592186078218 1145 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x + \\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\displaystyle \\int_0^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+18={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 12={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{10}(2f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{10}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(12)+x\\,{|}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =24+(10-0) \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534418 true] [17592186078227 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\displaystyle \\int_4^1 2 \\, f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = -1}$ \nand ${\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{-1} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2}.$  What is the value of ${\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^4 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx}?$" 13194139534418 true] [17592186078227 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\displaystyle \\int_4^1 2 \\, f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = -1}$\nand ${\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{-1} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2}$. What is the value of ${\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^4 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx}$?" 13194139534418 false] [17592186078227 1145 "Let's simplify the first given integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{4}^{1}2f(x)dx &= -1 \\\\\n2∫_{4}^{1}f(x)dx &= -1 \\\\\n- 2∫_{1}^{4}f(x)dx &= -1 \\\\\n∫_{1}^{4}f(x)dx &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{-1}f(x)dx &= 2 \\\\\n- ∫_{-1}^{1}f(x)dx &= 2 \\\\\n∫_{-1}^{1}f(x)dx &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{4}f(x)dx &= ∫_{-1}^{1}f(x)dx + ∫_{1}^{4}f(x)dx \\\\\n&=-2 + \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534418 false] [17592186078227 1145 "Let's simplify the first given integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{1}2\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ 2{\\int }_{4}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ -2{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ -{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534418 true] [17592186078346 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287174 13194139534418 true] [17592186078346 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287181 13194139534418 true] [17592186078346 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287188 13194139534418 true] [17592186078346 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287233 13194139534418 true] [17592186078346 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287237 13194139534418 true] [17592186078347 102